1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,696
What happens when a merchant's server asks for the same payment twice?

2
00:00:03,746 --> 00:00:09,576
In Lesson 24, every request carried a seal, and a copied request was refused.

3
00:00:09,626 --> 00:00:14,328
But a server may honestly send the same request twice, when an answer gets lost.

4
00:00:14,378 --> 00:00:19,656
This lesson makes that safe: however many times it is sent, one payment comes.

5
00:00:19,706 --> 00:00:21,792
Picture the shop's server at checkout.

6
00:00:21,842 --> 00:00:25,512
It sends the bank a request: create a payment for this order.

7
00:00:25,562 --> 00:00:27,384
Then the network hiccups.

8
00:00:27,434 --> 00:00:30,024
The request may have arrived, or it may not.

9
00:00:30,074 --> 00:00:31,896
The answer never comes back.

10
00:00:31,946 --> 00:00:34,488
The server cannot tell which half was lost.

11
00:00:34,538 --> 00:00:37,632
So it does the sensible thing and sends the request again.

12
00:00:37,682 --> 00:00:40,992
Without care, the bank now creates a second payment.

13
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:43,968
The buyer may get two payment pages for one order.

14
00:00:44,018 --> 00:00:46,488
A nervous buyer may pay both.

15
00:00:46,538 --> 00:00:50,328
Then the shop has to give money back, and the buyer has lost trust.

16
00:00:50,378 --> 00:00:52,200
Retrying is right.

17
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:56,040
The problem is a retry that the bank cannot recognise.

18
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:57,912
Think of a lift button.

19
00:00:57,962 --> 00:01:00,384
You press it once, and a lift is called.

20
00:01:00,434 --> 00:01:03,384
You press it ten more times, because you are in a hurry.

21
00:01:03,434 --> 00:01:05,256
Still one lift comes.

22
00:01:05,306 --> 00:01:08,400
Each press means the same thing: I want a lift here.

23
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:11,280
A payment request should work the same way.

24
00:01:11,330 --> 00:01:14,544
So each create carries an idempotency key.

25
00:01:14,594 --> 00:01:19,392
That is a plain name the merchant gives to this one attempt, such as the order and a counter.

26
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:24,192
The merchant chooses it, and it stays the same on every retry of that attempt.

27
00:01:24,242 --> 00:01:31,728
The bank remembers each key for each API client, together with the request it came with and the answer it gave.

28
00:01:31,778 --> 00:01:33,672
Now three things can happen.

29
00:01:33,722 --> 00:01:36,960
First, the same key arrives with the same request.

30
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:38,976
The bank does not create anything.

31
00:01:39,026 --> 00:01:43,632
It sends back the first answer again, with the same payment and the same page link.

32
00:01:43,682 --> 00:01:49,584
It marks that answer as an idempotent replay, so the merchant's server can see it is a repeat.

33
00:01:49,634 --> 00:01:54,696
Second, the same key arrives with a different request, for example a different amount.

34
00:01:54,746 --> 00:01:56,568
The bank refuses it.

35
00:01:56,618 --> 00:02:02,448
That is a different request under an old name, and guessing which one the merchant meant is never safe.

36
00:02:02,498 --> 00:02:06,000
Third, two retries arrive at the very same moment.

37
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:09,384
Both look new, because neither has been saved yet.

38
00:02:09,434 --> 00:02:14,928
They meet at one place in the bank's records, where each key can be written only once.

39
00:02:14,978 --> 00:02:17,208
One wins and creates the payment.

40
00:02:17,258 --> 00:02:20,112
The other loses, and gets the winner's answer back.

41
00:02:20,162 --> 00:02:22,632
There is one detail from the previous lesson.

42
00:02:22,682 --> 00:02:27,840
Every retry still needs a fresh seal, with a new time and a new one-time number.

43
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:32,784
So the server seals the request again, but keeps the same idempotency key.

44
00:02:32,834 --> 00:02:35,472
The seal stops copies made by strangers.

45
00:02:35,522 --> 00:02:38,688
The key ties together the retries made by the merchant.

46
00:02:38,738 --> 00:02:40,608
Now watch it from the merchant's server.

47
00:02:40,658 --> 00:02:42,744
It sends create payment with its key.

48
00:02:42,794 --> 00:02:44,616
The answer is lost on the way.

49
00:02:44,666 --> 00:02:48,168
It seals the same request again and sends it with the same key.

50
00:02:48,218 --> 00:02:50,040
This time the answer arrives.

51
00:02:50,090 --> 00:02:55,872
It holds the same payment and the same page link as the first, and it is marked as a replay.

52
00:02:55,922 --> 00:03:00,840
In the merchant portal, the payments list shows one payment for this order, not two.

53
00:03:00,890 --> 00:03:04,608
Now the server reuses the key, but with a different amount.

54
00:03:04,658 --> 00:03:09,720
The answer is a refusal that says the key was already used for another request.

55
00:03:09,770 --> 00:03:12,072
The list still shows one payment.

56
00:03:12,122 --> 00:03:13,944
What does this protect?

57
00:03:13,994 --> 00:03:17,280
It protects the buyer from paying twice for one order.

58
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:22,296
It protects the merchant from refunds and angry calls caused by a bad network.

59
00:03:22,346 --> 00:03:29,664
And it lets the merchant's developers write the simplest safe rule there is: when in doubt, send it again with the same key.

60
00:03:29,714 --> 00:03:35,400
Without the key, every timeout forces a hard choice between a lost order and a double charge.

61
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:38,016
With it, retrying is always safe.

62
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:39,888
So back to the lift button.

63
00:03:39,938 --> 00:03:42,840
Press it once, or press it ten times.

64
00:03:42,890 --> 00:03:45,480
One lift comes, and one payment is made.

65
00:03:45,530 --> 00:03:48,048
Next: the page the buyer opens.
